title the knife stays in the drawer -- keep every key, rewrite nothing, bind the emails account-side
prompt Keaton weighed regenerating keys and rewriting commit history with a new email against keeping the existing keys, owning both addresses.
think The ruling recommended and argued: every signature in history is cryptographically sound under keys he owns, and the only lag was GitHub's account-side binding of emails to keys -- which is retroactive, so fixing the binding flips every past badge with zero commits touched. A rewrite would force six running clones to re-clone mid-flight and move every hash the ledger froze 532 commit-body citations against, for no cryptographic gain; the debride law keeps that knife for dead tissue. Two independent one-time cures, either sufficient: the gh delete-and-re-add of the freshly UID-fixed key, and verifying the elder kaexvx9 address on the account so the key's original UID binds too. Going forward the fleet keeps per-clone keys by design -- the Seatbelt key is jail-scoped on purpose -- and uniform identity, if wanted, arrives by one quick-add-uid line per host keyring, never regeneration. Evidence beside the argument: grain-mystery's hand commit verified first try, so only grain-mind's key was ever the stray.
obs Counsel only; no tree change this lap beyond this record.
loom rewrite=declined keys_kept=all cures=2 citations_protected=532
file session-logs/date/README-index-20260830.md the shelf takes this row
